Z||000003||ThatRickGuy||05-21-2001||02:44 PM||||could have just been exhaust or a heat shield that had broken loose. I'd check the engine and watch for looseness, the motor mounts may be dead. if it's holding steady and the noise is gone, well, make sure your bolts are tight and enjoy until it rattles again.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||05-21-2001||03:22 PM||||kinda reminds me of my friend and his mom's Gremlin that he drove around when we were 18.<P>It always irked him that he couldnt spin the wheels at all. At least I could spin my tires in reverse if I popped the clutch on my VW. He had something like a 200CU engine and 1 barrel carb with auto tranny. When he tramped down on the pedal it was like pushing the throttle forward on a ChrisCraft cabin cruiser.<P>One day he decided he was gonna lite up them tires, no mater what. So he put it in neutral and reved it up to about 4000rpm and slammed it into 1st.<P>  He took off down the street, tires screeching like crazy, and there was a hugh trail of green water flowing from under the car, leaving a trail behind him. And this gaudawful grinding scraping chattering noise.<P>  He had finally managed to spin the tires. He also managed to rip all the engine mounts loose. He also managed to put the radiator fan halfway through the radiator as the drive train way busy trying to escape out the front grill.<P>   His mom was 'not amused'.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||05-21-2001||03:27 PM||||now that I think about it, the same guy had a VW van a few years later. He was turning a corner in the city and the engine mounts on that let go. Because of the way the motor throttle cable is laid out, and the the gear selector rod in the tranny, when the engine came loose it slipped backwards and did two things:<P> it pulled the transmission into reverse<P>  and it pull the throttle cable WFO.<P>   He thought someone had a tractor beam on him, one minute hes going around the corner, the next minute hes going backwards around the corner, like time was going backwards, at full throttle, till the engine and tranny fell out completely.<P>  I remember after that he was asking where you can buy solid metal engine mounts.||||reg||1
Z||000006||penetrator||05-24-2001||07:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ThatRickGuy:<BR><B>could have just been exhaust or a heat shield that had broken loose. I'd check the engine and watch for looseness, the motor mounts may be dead. if it's holding steady and the noise is gone, well, make sure your bolts are tight and enjoy until it rattles again.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I've checked all parts that could have been loose, except the enginemounts. My guess is that because the dogbone was loose the angle of the engine was to extreme so the noise came either from the drive axels or the tranny. The noise sounded like u was trying to change gear without the clutch engaged. But i have no problems to put in the gears and the gear stick dosent move around either.<P>In a few days im going to raise the car and check all bolts and mounts. Ill be back with my new opinion then.<P>Thanks alot for all replys.<P>I dont Hope my car ends up like that Wolksvagen. =)<BR> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||thomas_l||05-24-2001||08:33 AM||||Check your exhaust mounts too. If you still don't find anything, change your trans lube and look for metal in the oil for a sign of internal problems. A little bit of fine dust is ok - bigger pieces wouldn't be a good sign  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||3
